// SKIP AND TAKE ARE THE SLICE, AND ON THE PERSISTENT CARRIER THEY SHARE THE RECEIVER'S TREE. A walk
// that reads a code-point or octet list by offset slices it as `xs |> skip(n: a) |> take(n: b - a)`;
// when either half copied its result, each slice paid the remainder and the walk was quadratic in
// the list (DESIGN section 6, bare minimum cost). Both realizations hold the list as an RRB vector
// (the interpreter's Value::List, v1_rt's im::Vector), whose split is logarithmic, so the slice
// costs log len to form and only its own length to read. That removes the quadratic remainder
// copying; it does not make an offset walk linear, since every member read is get_contract's
// log n on this carrier, so a walk that reads each offset is O(n log n).
//
// THE COUNT IS CLAMPED, AND A NEGATIVE COUNT IS THE WHOLE LIST. Both realizations take
// k = if n < 0 then len else min(n, len): skip keeps len - k members and take keeps k.
data skip_contract: PrimitiveContract = {
  name: "skip",
  work: "log len",
  output_size: "len - k where k = if n < 0 then len else min(n, len)",
  certainty: Proven,
  carrier_cost: CarrierSensitive { ephemeral_work: "len - k where k = if n < 0 then len else min(n, len)", persistent_work: "log len" }
}

data take_contract: PrimitiveContract = {
  name: "take",
  work: "log len",
  output_size: "k where k = if n < 0 then len else min(n, len)",
  certainty: Proven,
  carrier_cost: CarrierSensitive { ephemeral_work: "k where k = if n < 0 then len else min(n, len)", persistent_work: "log len" }
}

